ted States again entered a team of NBA stars, with several players returning from the Dream Team – notably " [:a {:href "/athletes/5678"} "Charles Barkley"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/6972"} "David Robinson"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/6586"} "Karl Malone"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/7175"} "John Stockton"] ". The team was dubbed by the media as “Dream Team II” but it was a lesser version of the 1992 leviathan, and in addition, the rest of the world was now beginning to catch up to the United States in basketball – even to the professional version."] "\n\n" [:p {} "After the break-up of the Soviet Union and Yugoslavia, the former European powers were now slightly different. Though Russia had a good team, it did not qualify in 1996, and the best team among the former Soviet republics belonged to Lithuania, led by NBA stars " [:a {:href "/athletes/6595"} "Šarūnas Marčiulionis"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/7013"} "Arvydas Sabonis"] ". Yugoslavia contributed two top contenders in Croatia and the re-formed Yugoslavia, now represented by Serbia &amp; Montenegro, with all of the players coming from Serbia."] "\n\n" [:p {} "But in Atlanta, the United States won fairly comfortably. They dominated Group A, and won the quarter-finals against Brazil, 98-75; the semi-finals against Australia, 101-73; and the final against Yugoslavia, 95-69."] "\n"] [:button {:id "button505591", :class "btn btn-default"} "Details"] [:script {} "$('#button505591').click(splits)"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {}] [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Team"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {:data-toggle "tooltip", :data-placement "right", :data-original-title "Games Played", :class "split", :style "display: none"} "GP"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Points"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Scoring Average"] [:th {:data-toggle "tooltip", :data-placement "right", :data-original-title "Time Played", :class "split", :style "display: none"} 